Using VS Code GUI

Open your repository in VS Code.
Select the current branch in the Status Bar at the bottom left or open the Command Palette (Ctrl+Shift+P / Cmd+Shift+P) and run Git: 
    Create Branch.
Enter a descriptive branch name, e.g., feature/user-authentication or bugfix/login-error 

1.
Choose the source branch (usually main or develop) from which to create the new branch.
VS Code will switch to the new branch automatically.
To publish the branch to GitHub, click Publish Branch in the Source Control view 

2.
If you have the GitHub Pull Requests and Issues extension, you can also create a branch directly from a GitHub issue, which pre-fills the pull request for you 

3.
Using VS Code Terminal
Open the integrated terminal in VS Code.
Make sure your current branch is up-to-date:
git pull origin main
Create and switch to a new branch in one step:
git checkout -b new-branch-name
Alternatively, you can create and switch in two steps:
git branch new-branch-name
git checkout new-branch-name
Push the new branch to GitHub:
git push -u origin new-branch-name
The -u flag sets the upstream so future git push and git pull commands default to this branch 


4.
Best Practices
Use descriptive branch names to indicate the purpose of the branch.
Always pull the latest changes from the remote branch before creating a new branch to avoid conflicts.
Merge your feature branch back into main or develop once your work is complete, using Git: Merge Branch in VS Code or the terminal.
Delete branches that are no longer needed to keep your repository clean, either locally or remotely 


5.
By following these steps, you can efficiently create, switch, and push new branches in VS Code while keeping your GitHub repository organized and up-to-date.